== Chapter XVI

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Showing that all must be traced back to God’s purpose.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#smallcaps[Let] us ascribe the whole work of grace to the good pleasure of God’s will#footnote[#emph[Use.] 1.]. God did not choose us because we were worthy, but by choosing us he makes us worthy. Proud men are apt to claim and take too much to themselves, as if they shared the work with God. While many cry out against sacrilege against the church, they are meanwhile guilty of a far greater sacrilege: they rob God of his glory when they set out to put the #emph[crown of salvation] on their own head. But we must trace everything back to God’s #emph[purpose.] The signs of salvation are in the saints, but the cause of salvation is in God. If it is God’s purpose that saves, then:

1\. It is not #emph[free will.] The #emph[Pelagians] are strenuous defenders of free will. They tell us that a man has an inborn power for his own conversion. But this text refutes it: our calling is #emph[according to God’s purpose.] The Scripture pulls up the weed of free will by the roots (Rom 9:16): “It does not depend on man’s will.” Everything depends on the πρόθεσις, the #emph[purpose] of God. When the prisoner is condemned at the bar, there is no saving him unless the King has a purpose to save him. God’s purpose is his royal prerogative.

2\. If it is God’s purpose that saves, then it is not #emph[merit.] #emph[Bellarmine] holds that good works atone for sin and merit glory. No, the text says we are called according to #emph[God’s purpose;] and there is a parallel Scripture, 2 Tim 1:9: “He has saved us and called us …, not because of our works, but by His own purpose and grace.” There is no such thing as merit. Our best works have in them both defect and infection, and so they are only #emph[splendida peccata,] glittering sins. So if we are called and justified, it is God’s purpose that brings it about.

#emph[Objection.] But the papists cite this Scripture for merit, 2 Tim 4:8: “From now on there is laid up for me the crown of righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, will award to me on that day.” This is the force of their argument: if God in justice rewards our works, then they merit salvation.

#emph[Response.] To this I answer, first, that God gives a reward as a just Judge, not to the worthiness of our works, but to the worthiness of Christ. Second, God as a just Judge rewards us, not because we have deserved it, but because he has promised it.#footnote[#emph[Debitorem se fecit Dominus, non accipiendo, sed promittendo.] \[The Lord made himself a debtor, not by receiving, but by promising.\] Augustine.]\; God has two courts, a court of mercy and a court of justice. The Lord condemns in the court of justice those works which he crowns in the court of mercy. So what carries the main stroke in our salvation is the πρόθεσις, the #emph[purpose] of God.

3\. If the purpose of God is the fountainhead of happiness, then we are not saved for #emph[faith foreseen.] It is absurd to think that anything in us could have the least influence on our election#footnote[#emph[Opera praevisa non póssunt esse causae Praedestinationis.] \[Works foreseen cannot be causes of predestination.\] Peter Martyr.]. The #emph[Arminians] say that God foresaw that such persons would believe, and therefore chose them; and so they would make the business of salvation depend on something in us. But God does not choose us for faith, but to faith (Eph 1:4): “He chose us … to be holy.” Not because we would be holy, but so that we might be holy. We are elected to holiness, not for it#footnote[#emph[Deus elegit fideles ut sint, non quia jam erant.] \[God chose believers so that they might be such, not because they already were.\] Augustine, On the Predestination of the Saints.]. What could God foresee in us but pollution and rebellion? If any man is saved, it is κατὰ πρόθεσιν, #emph[according to God’s purpose.]

#emph[Question.] How shall we know that God has a purpose to save us?

#emph[Answer.] By being effectually called. 2 Pet 1:10: “Strive to make your calling and election sure.” We make our #emph[election] sure by making our #emph[calling] sure. 2 Thess 2:13: “God has chosen you#super[+] … to be saved by the sanctification of the Spirit.” By the stream we come at last to the fountain. If we find the stream of sanctification running in our souls, we may by this come to the fountainhead of election. When a man’s eyes are so weak that he cannot look up to the sky, he may still know the moon is there by seeing it shine on the water. So though I do not look up into the secret of God’s purpose, I may know I am elected by the shining of sanctifying grace in my soul. Whoever finds the word of God transcribed and copied out into his heart may conclude beyond denial that he is elected.#footnote[#emph[Use.] 2.]

=== The Saints’ Comforts Built upon This Impregnable Rock

#emph[Use.] 2. Here is a sovereign #emph[elixir,] an unspeakable comfort for those who are the called of God: their salvation rests on God’s purpose (2 Tim 2:19): “God’s firm foundation stands, bearing this seal: ‘The Lord knows those who are His.’” Our graces are imperfect; our comforts ebb and flow; but #emph[God’s firm foundation stands.] Those who are built on this rock of God’s #emph[eternal purpose] need not fear falling away. Neither the power of man nor the violence of temptation will ever be able to overturn them.
